\section{Cross-Scene Validation: spatial\_bowl}
\label{sec:appendix_spatial_bowl}

To test whether the SDF redundancy finding generalizes beyond the primary \texttt{object\_pudding} scene, we replicate the core comparison on \texttt{spatial\_bowl}, a separate SafeLIBERO suite with a distinct obstacle layout (moka pot, wine bottle, milk carton, red coffee mug, yellow book). Both conditions use the same architecture, hyperparameters, and two-phase training schedule (30K steps), differing only in the training scene.

Table~\ref{tab:spatial_bowl} reports the results. The SDF condition ($\lambda = 0.005$) yields CAR of 17.0\% versus 15.5\% for LoRA-only, a $+$1.5\,pp difference that falls within the seed-to-seed noise observed in the main experiments ($\sigma \approx 1.9$\,pp). TSR is comparable across conditions (50.0\% vs.\ 53.0\%). Both evaluations use a single training seed (seed~0) and a single evaluation seed (seed~7, 200 episodes), so statistical power is limited; nevertheless, the qualitative pattern---SDF failing to separate from LoRA-only---is consistent with the \texttt{object\_pudding} findings.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-scene validation on \texttt{spatial\_bowl} (single training seed, 200 episodes, 30K checkpoint). The $+$1.5\,pp CAR difference is within the seed-to-seed range observed in \texttt{object\_pudding}.}
\label{tab:spatial_bowl}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Condition & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) \\
\midrule
LoRA-only & 15.5 & 50.0 \\
SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & 17.0 & 53.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The substantially lower CAR on \texttt{spatial\_bowl} relative to \texttt{object\_pudding} (15--17\% vs.\ 54--57\%) reflects the greater scene difficulty: \texttt{spatial\_bowl} places five cluttered obstacles around the workspace, making collision-free manipulation considerably harder. This difficulty affects both conditions equally, and the SDF advantage remains negligible ($\Delta$CAR\,=\,$+$1.5\,pp), consistent with the conclusion that SDF auxiliary supervision does not produce a meaningful collision avoidance benefit beyond LoRA-only fine-tuning.


\section{Training Details}
\label{sec:appendix_training}

\paragraph{Architecture.}
We use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} adapters injected into the PaliGemma vision encoder (rank 16, $\alpha$=16) and the action expert (rank 32, $\alpha$=32), following the configuration of OpenPi. The SDF decoder is a 4-layer MLP (hidden dimension 256) with skip connections, taking pooled suffix features (1024-d) and query points (3-d) as input. The end-effector extractor is a 2-layer MLP (1024 $\to$ 256 $\to$ 3).

\paragraph{Training schedule.}
Training proceeds in two phases:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Phase 1} (steps 0--5000): Backbone and LoRA parameters are frozen. Only the SDF decoder and EE extractor are trained, allowing them to learn meaningful representations before the policy adapts.
    \item \textbf{Phase 2} (steps 5000--30000): LoRA parameters are unfrozen with a 100-step gradient warmup ramp. All auxiliary heads continue training jointly with the policy.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Training hyperparameters.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Base model & Pi0.5 (OpenPi, $\sim$3.5B parameters) \\
Optimizer & AdamW \\
Learning rate & $2.5 \times 10^{-5}$ (cosine decay to $\mathrm{lr}/10$) \\
Warmup steps & 1000 \\
Batch size & 32 \\
Total steps & 30000 \\
$\lambda_\mathrm{sdf}$ & \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\} \\
$\lambda_\mathrm{ee}$ & 0.01 \\
SDF query points per step & 64 \\
SDF clamp $\delta$ & 0.05 m \\
Eikonal weight & 0.1 \\
LoRA rank (vision) & 16 \\
LoRA rank (action) & 32 \\
Phase 1 duration & 5000 steps \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Evaluation protocol.}
Each episode runs for at most 300 timesteps. The policy generates a 50-step action trajectory via flow matching and executes the first 5 steps before replanning. An episode terminates early upon task completion. Collision detection uses MuJoCo's built-in contact detection: any contact between the robot and a non-target object registers as a collision. For Pi0.5 evaluations, camera images are rotated 180$^\circ$ to match the pre-training viewpoint convention; this correction was verified to restore TSR from ${\sim}$5\% to ${\sim}$52\% while leaving CAR comparable. Oracle variants use 40 episodes per task (160 total) with a single seed; all other configurations use 50 episodes per task (200 total).

\paragraph{Data.}
We use the SafeLIBERO object\_pudding suite with pre-computed SDF ground truth. SDF values are computed offline from the obstacle mesh using a dense $64^3$ voxel grid. During training, 64 query points are randomly sampled per step from this grid.

\paragraph{Computational resources.}
All training and evaluation experiments are conducted on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96\,GB). Phase~1 (5K steps) takes approximately 6 hours on a single GPU; Phase~2 (25K steps) takes approximately 32 hours. Evaluation of 200 episodes requires approximately 4 hours. Across 100+ experiments (training runs, evaluations, oracle variants, and ablations), the total computational budget is approximately 700 GPU-hours.

